[h1]Heroes Evolve[/h1]

In vanilla, AI lords keep the gear they were made with until they die. With this
mod they evolve: they learn with the years, buy better gear in town markets and
take it off each other after battles.

[list]
[*]Every system can be turned off on its own.
[*]Nothing is written to your save: add it to a campaign in progress, or remove
it, whenever you like.
[/list]

[h2]Starting Kit[/h2]
[list]
[*]Fixes a TaleWorlds bug: some lords come of age in civilian clothes or with a
single weapon, and stay like that for life.
[*]They get a basic kit for their culture, role and age. Anything better, they
buy themselves.
[*]Only those lords, and only once. A lord the game equips properly is never
touched.
[/list]

[h2]Not every lord fights like their culture[/h2]
[list]
[*]Now and then a lord comes of age armed for what they are good at, not for
what their culture teaches: a Vlandian crossbowman on foot, a Khuzait archer with
no horse, a Sturgian javelin rider.
[*]Always with gear their culture's troops really use, so it works with modded
cultures too. Never a Vlandian archer, a Battanian horse archer or a mounted
Nord.
[/list]

[h2]Skills grow over time[/h2]
[list]
[*]You can choose in the options between our growth system, explained below, and
the vanilla one.
[*]Lords, companions and wanderers improve in the weapons they carry and the
trades they have focus points in. The weapon with the most focus grows fastest.
[*]Every hero has a hidden talent for combat, management and seafaring, fixed for
life. It decides how fast they learn and how far they get. A great fighter can
be a poor steward.
[*]Young lords start green and grow into veterans. The lords TaleWorlds wrote by
hand reach their written skills at about 55, and go 5% beyond at 60.
[*]In a new campaign, every lord starts with the skills of their age, and so does
any lord who appears later. No 20-year-old fights like a 50-year-old. Campaigns
already under way are left alone.
[*]Lords hit by the Starting Kit bug also get the combat skills of their age.
[*]About 1 hero in 200 ends up stronger than the best lord in vanilla. It could
be a companion you hired.
[/list]

[h2]Lords buy their own gear[/h2]
[list]
[*]In a town, a lord buys better gear with their clan's money.
[*]Their skills set the limit: a weak lord cannot buy top-tier armour.
[*]They only upgrade what they already use: no new weapon types, no filling empty
slots, no horse for someone who fights on foot, camels only in cultures that
ride them.
[*]Equip a companion and give them a party: they improve that loadout instead of
replacing it.
[*]The worst piece of armour goes first.
[*]They prefer their own culture's gear (+25%), or buy only that if you choose.
[*]Rich clans dress their lords better, but always keep enough to pay their
troops.
[*]Among pieces of about the same quality, each lord follows their own taste, so
a rich kingdom never ends up dressed alike.
[/list]

[h2]Caravan masters buy their own armour[/h2]
[list]
[*]They take a commission on what the caravan earns and buy gear with it. Your
treasury is never touched.
[*]They start with nothing and never spend more than they have earned.
[*]You set the commission: higher means a better armed master and less profit
for you. They take nothing once there is nothing left to improve.
[*]Bandits who beat the caravan capture its master and strip them.
[/list]

[h2]Robbery[/h2]
[list]
[*]Hold a lord prisoner and you can take their weapons and armour, unique gear
included.
[*]AI lords rob their prisoners too. The gear stays in the world: they wear it
or sell it, and you can win it back by beating them.
[*]Nobody is left naked: a robbed hero is instantly given basic gear of the same
kinds, mount included.
[*]Loadouts survive capture: lose a companion you equipped as a crossbowman and
they come back with a crossbow. Those who fought on foot stay on foot.
[*]Heroes already in rags are not worth robbing and are left alone.
[*]You get a message whenever someone in your clan or kingdom robs or is robbed,
naming who did it.
[*]Price: -12 relation with their clan and half a level of honour. Half that for
a dishonourable prisoner, unless they are your friend. AI lords pay the same
relation.
[*][b]Vengeance system.[/b] Rob a hero and their clan swears vengeance: when they
capture someone from the robber's clan, they strip them, once for each robbery.
AI clans do it to each other and to you, and you can do it to them without
losing honour or relation. The encyclopedia shows who has sworn against whom.
[*]Lose your honour and honourable lords will strip you as a matter of justice.
[*]How: with a prisoner in your party or in a settlement your clan owns, talk to
them and choose [i]"Hand over your arms and armour."[/i] They answer before
anything is taken, and you can still change your mind.
[/list]

How likely an AI lord is to rob depends on their character. Each trait alone,
with the other three neutral, at default settings:

[code]
                 -2      -1       0      +1      +2
Honour         15.8%   10.3%    6.2%    3.4%    1.6%
Generosity     12.9%    9.2%    6.2%    4.0%    2.4%
Mercy          12.9%    9.2%    6.2%    4.0%    2.4%
Calculating     8.1%    7.1%    6.2%    5.4%    4.7%
[/code]

[list]
[*]Traits add up: all four at -1 is 21.1%, all neutral 6.2%, all at +1 0.8%.
[*]Roguery adds up to +50%. Relation +100 means never, -100 adds +50%. Own clan
x0.25. Close family never, unless relation is under -50. Bandits always 50%.
[*]A clan owed vengeance always strips. Honourable lords strip dishonourable
prisoners more: 17.4% at Honour -1, 31.4% at -2.
[*]The [b]How often lords rob[/b] slider multiplies all of it: 0 means AI lords
never rob (you still can), 1 is the default, 2 doubles everything.
[/list]

[h2]Keeping other mods' gear out[/h2]
[list]
[*]Gear packs add hundreds of top-tier items, and one pack can end up dressing the
whole map.
[*]In MCM, [i]Heroes Evolve - Excluded gear[/i] lists every gear mod you have,
each with a tick box. Tick one and AI lords never buy or receive its items. You
and your troops can still use them.
[*]You can also exclude single items by id on the same screen.
[/list]

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[list]
[*]Mod Configuration Menu (MCM) v5, for the in-game options screen.
[/list]

[h2]Configuration[/h2]
[list]
[*]Most changes apply at once: no restart, no new campaign.
[*]Set how freely lords spend, whose culture they prefer, how often they rob, the
top gear tier, the caravan commission, the growth system and which items or mods
are left out.
[*]Choose whether the mod manages your own clan or leaves it all to you.
[*]A console command writes a report on every lord in your campaign to a log.
[/list]

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
[list]
[*]Works with mods that add items or cultures.
[*]Built and tested with War Sails, but does not need it.
[nexus-only]
[*]Two main files: v1.7.0 for Bannerlord v1.5.7, and v1.5.3 for players staying on
v1.4.8.
[/nexus-only]
[steam-only]
[*]Staying on Bannerlord v1.4.8? Unsubscribe here and install Heroes Evolve v1.5.3
from Nexus, which still supports it.
[/steam-only]
[*]Using RBM? Turn off its [i]AI Lords Buy Equipment[/i] option. Otherwise both
mods buy gear for lords, and RBM ignores your excluded gear.
[/list]

[nexus-only]
[h2]Installation[/h2]

[list=1]
[*]Drop the [i]HeroesEvolve[/i] folder into
[i]Mount & Blade II Bannerlord/Modules/[/i].
[*]Tick Heroes Evolve in the launcher's module list.
[*]Load order sorts itself: the module declares what it needs.
[/list]

[/nexus-only]
[h2]Languages[/h2]

English, Spanish, French, German, Russian, Turkish, Chinese (Simplified and
Traditional, by Cartoonist57).
